  {
    "@id": "https://w3id.org/smn/CycleLine",
    "@type": [
      "http://www.w3.org/2004/02/skos/core#Concept"
    ],
    "http://purl.obolibrary.org/obo/IAO_0000119": [
      {
        "@language": "en",
        "@value": "The construct, and the invariance condition it depends on, are the broodline of Holtby, L.B. and Ciruna, K.A. 2007, Conservation Units for Pacific Salmon under the Wild Salmon Policy, DFO Canadian Science Advisory Secretariat Research Document 2007/070, glossary: in a semelparous species, if the age of reproduction is fixed or nearly so, the fish spawning in sequential generations are called broodlines, and in Pink Salmon there are even-year and odd-year broodlines in most rivers. That document treats the two as separate races for establishing Conservation Units. The name cycle line is this vocabulary's, not that document's, which uses broodline throughout; see the scope note on smn:CycleLine."
      }
    ],
    "http://purl.org/dc/terms/source": [
      {
        "@id": "https://www.dfo-mpo.gc.ca/csas-sccs/publications/resdocs-docrech/2007/2007_070-eng.htm"
      }
    ],
    "http://www.w3.org/2000/01/rdf-schema#isDefinedBy": [
      {
        "@id": "https://w3id.org/smn"
      }
    ],
    "http://www.w3.org/2004/02/skos/core#altLabel": [
      {
        "@language": "en",
        "@value": "Broodline"
      }
    ],
    "http://www.w3.org/2004/02/skos/core#definition": [
      {
        "@language": "en",
        "@value": "One year series of a salmon population's returns, comprising the years congruent to one another modulo the population's cycle period under a declared year basis."
      }
    ],
    "http://www.w3.org/2004/02/skos/core#inScheme": [
      {
        "@id": "https://w3id.org/smn/CycleLineScheme"
      }
    ],
    "http://www.w3.org/2004/02/skos/core#prefLabel": [
      {
        "@language": "en",
        "@value": "Cycle line"
      }
    ],
    "http://www.w3.org/2004/02/skos/core#scopeNote": [
      {
        "@language": "en",
        "@value": "Naming caution, recorded because it is unresolved: cycle line is this vocabulary's coinage. Holtby and Ciruna (2007), the framework this scheme draws on, says broodline and never says cycle line, and in Fraser Sockeye practice a cycle line is one of the four lines of the cyclic-dominance model — which is the model this concept defers. The term therefore currently names a construct in a scheme that excludes it. Cyclic dominance is a separate, deferred model and is not represented here: whether a line is dominant, subdominant, or off-cycle is a time-scoped status a line holds in one system, not the identity of the line. Its successor is named rather than left open — DFO's three Conservation Unit fields Cyclic (a boolean of the CU), Cyc_Dom, and Cyc_Dom_Year (which line, for that CU, in that period), whose values are dominant, subdominant, and off-cycle. Modelling those retires this deferral. Note that the fields cut across this scheme rather than along it: DFO records Fraser Pink as Cyclic = FALSE while still assigning it a cycle line."
      }
    ],
    "http://www.w3.org/2004/02/skos/core#topConceptOf": [
      {
        "@id": "https://w3id.org/smn/CycleLineScheme"
      }
    ]
  },
  {
    "@id": "https://w3id.org/smn/CycleLineScheme",
    "@type": [
      "http://www.w3.org/2004/02/skos/core#ConceptScheme"
    ],
    "http://purl.obolibrary.org/obo/IAO_0000119": [
      {
        "@language": "en",
        "@value": "The construct, and the invariance condition it depends on, are the broodline of Holtby, L.B. and Ciruna, K.A. 2007, Conservation Units for Pacific Salmon under the Wild Salmon Policy, DFO Canadian Science Advisory Secretariat Research Document 2007/070, glossary: in a semelparous species, if the age of reproduction is fixed or nearly so, the fish spawning in sequential generations are called broodlines, and in Pink Salmon there are even-year and odd-year broodlines in most rivers. That document treats the two as separate races for establishing Conservation Units. The name cycle line is this vocabulary's, not that document's, which uses broodline throughout; see the scope note on smn:CycleLine."
      }
    ],
    "http://purl.org/dc/terms/source": [
      {
        "@id": "https://www.dfo-mpo.gc.ca/csas-sccs/publications/resdocs-docrech/2007/2007_070-eng.htm"
      }
    ],
    "http://www.w3.org/2000/01/rdf-schema#isDefinedBy": [
      {
        "@id": "https://w3id.org/smn"
      }
    ],
    "http://www.w3.org/2004/02/skos/core#definition": [
      {
        "@language": "en",
        "@value": "Controlled vocabulary for cycle lines: the year series a population's returns fall into when the years are partitioned modulo the population's cycle period, under a declared year basis."
      }
    ],
    "http://www.w3.org/2004/02/skos/core#hasTopConcept": [
      {
        "@id": "https://w3id.org/smn/CycleLine"
      }
    ],
    "http://www.w3.org/2004/02/skos/core#prefLabel": [
      {
        "@language": "en",
        "@value": "Cycle line scheme"
      }
    ],
    "http://www.w3.org/2004/02/skos/core#scopeNote": [
      {
        "@language": "en",
        "@value": "The construct is a year series, not a lineage. Reproductive independence is an additional claim that holds only where age at return is invariant, and it is asserted with smn:hasCycleLine rather than built into these definitions. Pink Salmon return at age 2 invariantly, so the brood-year and return-year partitions coincide and each line is closed under reproduction. Fraser Sockeye are the counter-case: roughly 89 to 92 percent return at age 4 but real age-3 and age-5 components exist, so a fish spawned on one line can return on another, the class is not closed, and the brood-year and return-year partitions of the same fish are not equivalent. Declaring a year basis from smn:YearBasisScheme therefore selects which assertion is being made; it does not reconcile the two."
      }
    ]
  },
