  {
    "@id": "https://w3id.org/smn/SockeyeLakeTypeLifeHistory",
    "@type": [
      "http://www.w3.org/2004/02/skos/core#Concept"
    ],
    "http://purl.obolibrary.org/obo/IAO_0000119": [
      {
        "@language": "en",
        "@value": "Beacham, T.D. and Withler, R.E. 2017, Population structure of sea-type and lake-type sockeye salmon and kokanee in the Fraser River and Columbia River drainages, PLOS ONE 12(9):e0183713. Used as a Conservation Unit delimiting axis by Holtby, L.B. and Ciruna, K.A. 2007, DFO Can. Sci. Advis. Sec. Res. Doc. 2007/070. Burgner, R.L. 1991, Life history of sockeye salmon (Oncorhynchus nerka), in Groot, C. and Margolis, L. (eds.), Pacific Salmon Life Histories, UBC Press, pages 3-117, is the customary citation for the lake-type form specifically; it is recorded here rather than relied on, because the chapter could not be read."
      }
    ],
    "http://purl.org/dc/terms/source": [
      {
        "@id": "https://doi.org/10.1371/journal.pone.0183713"
      }
    ],
    "http://rs.tdwg.org/dwc/terms/scientificName": [
      {
        "@value": "Oncorhynchus nerka"
      }
    ],
    "http://rs.tdwg.org/dwc/terms/scientificNameID": [
      {
        "@value": "urn:lsid:marinespecies.org:taxname:254569"
      }
    ],
    "http://www.w3.org/2000/01/rdf-schema#isDefinedBy": [
      {
        "@id": "https://w3id.org/smn"
      }
    ],
    "http://www.w3.org/2000/01/rdf-schema#seeAlso": [
      {
        "@id": "https://www.marinespecies.org/aphia.php?p=taxdetails&id=254569"
      }
    ],
    "http://www.w3.org/2004/02/skos/core#altLabel": [
      {
        "@language": "en",
        "@value": "Lake Type"
      }
    ],
    "http://www.w3.org/2004/02/skos/core#broader": [
      {
        "@id": "https://w3id.org/smn/LifeHistoryType"
      }
    ],
    "http://www.w3.org/2004/02/skos/core#definition": [
      {
        "@language": "en",
        "@value": "Life-history type of Sockeye Salmon in which juveniles rear in a nursery lake for at least one year before migrating to sea: yearling-or-older freshwater residence combined with lake rearing habitat."
      }
    ],
    "http://www.w3.org/2004/02/skos/core#inScheme": [
      {
        "@id": "https://w3id.org/smn/LifeHistoryTypeScheme"
      }
    ],
    "http://www.w3.org/2004/02/skos/core#prefLabel": [
      {
        "@language": "en",
        "@value": "Lake-type Sockeye life history"
      }
    ],
    "http://www.w3.org/2004/02/skos/core#scopeNote": [
      {
        "@language": "en",
        "@value": "This is the one Sockeye type whose DFO Conservation Unit code is unambiguous: SEL decomposes into this concept plus the species, and nothing else. Its sibling code SER does not — see smn:SockeyeRiverTypeLifeHistory. The altLabel is the bare string used in source data; outside the SEL context that string does not name a species, which is why the concept does."
      }
    ],
    "https://w3id.org/smn/hasJuvenileFreshwaterResidenceDuration": [
      {
        "@id": "https://w3id.org/smn/YearlingFreshwaterResidence"
      }
    ],
    "https://w3id.org/smn/hasJuvenileRearingHabitat": [
      {
        "@id": "https://w3id.org/smn/LakeRearingHabitat"
      }
    ]
  },
  {
    "@id": "https://w3id.org/smn/SockeyeRiverTypeLifeHistory",
    "@type": [
      "http://www.w3.org/2004/02/skos/core#Concept"
    ],
    "http://purl.obolibrary.org/obo/IAO_0000119": [
      {
        "@language": "en",
        "@value": "The label river-type sockeye traces to Semko 1954, per Wood, C.C., Riddell, B.E., and Rutherford, D.T. 1987, Can. Spec. Publ. Fish. Aquat. Sci. 96:12-24, and Wood, C.C., Bickham, J.W., Nelson, R.J., Foote, C.J., and Patton, J.C. 2008, Recurrent evolution of life history ecotypes in sockeye salmon, Evolutionary Applications 1:207-221. The one-to-two-year riverine rearing sense: DFO Can. Sci. Advis. Sec. Sci. Advis. Rep. 2022/003, Recovery Potential Assessment for Fraser River Sockeye Salmon, Nine Designatable Units, Part 2. The breadth of DFO's SER code is from the Conservation Unit data dictionary published with the Pacific salmon Conservation Unit datasets, verified 2026-08-25. Attribution caution: Pavey et al. 2010 inverts the Semko and Gilbert credits between river-type and sea-type; Wood et al. 1987 is earlier and is followed here."
      }
    ],
    "http://purl.org/dc/terms/source": [
      {
        "@id": "https://doi.org/10.1111/j.1752-4571.2008.00028.x"
      }
    ],
    "http://rs.tdwg.org/dwc/terms/scientificName": [
      {
        "@value": "Oncorhynchus nerka"
      }
    ],
    "http://rs.tdwg.org/dwc/terms/scientificNameID": [
      {
        "@value": "urn:lsid:marinespecies.org:taxname:254569"
      }
    ],
    "http://www.w3.org/2000/01/rdf-schema#isDefinedBy": [
      {
        "@id": "https://w3id.org/smn"
      }
    ],
    "http://www.w3.org/2000/01/rdf-schema#seeAlso": [
      {
        "@id": "https://www.marinespecies.org/aphia.php?p=taxdetails&id=254569"
      }
    ],
    "http://www.w3.org/2004/02/skos/core#altLabel": [
      {
        "@language": "en",
        "@value": "River Type"
      }
    ],
    "http://www.w3.org/2004/02/skos/core#broader": [
      {
        "@id": "https://w3id.org/smn/LifeHistoryType"
      }
    ],
    "http://www.w3.org/2004/02/skos/core#definition": [
      {
        "@language": "en",
        "@value": "Life-history type of Sockeye Salmon in which juveniles rear for one to two years in flowing water, or an associated slough, rather than in a lake, and so carry at least one freshwater annulus: yearling-or-older freshwater residence combined with riverine rearing habitat."
      }
    ],
    "http://www.w3.org/2004/02/skos/core#inScheme": [
      {
        "@id": "https://w3id.org/smn/LifeHistoryTypeScheme"
      }
    ],
    "http://www.w3.org/2004/02/skos/core#prefLabel": [
      {
        "@language": "en",
        "@value": "River-type Sockeye life history"
      }
    ],
    "http://www.w3.org/2004/02/skos/core#scopeNote": [
      {
        "@language": "en",
        "@value": "This concept is NOT the whole of the DFO Conservation Unit species code SER, and mapping SER onto it alone is wrong. DFO's Conservation Unit data dictionary glosses SER as \"River or Ocean Type Sockeye Salmon\" (French: \"saumon rouge de type riviere ou ocean\"), so the code's non-species half is a disjunction of this concept and smn:SockeyeSeaTypeLifeHistory, which take opposite values on the freshwater residence duration axis. DFO's Conservation Unit lookup renders the same code with the shorter label RIVER TYPE SOCKEYE SALMON and files under it both WIDGEON (SER-02) and HARRISON RIVER (SER-03), which are the two populations the Sockeye literature identifies as sea-type; DFO CSAS Science Advisory Report 2022/003 states of designatable unit DU24, Widgeon-RT, that \"it is not a true river-type population; these fish migrate to sea in their first year and do not overwinter in freshwater stream habitat\", and calls it the only ocean-type population in that assessment. The breadth is deliberate and traceable rather than a slip: Holtby and Ciruna (2007), the framework DFO cites for its Conservation Units, names the second Sockeye life-history type the \"ocean-, stream- or river-type\", says its juveniles rear in flowing water and may smolt soon after emergence, and glosses it in the document glossary as juveniles adapted to rearing in flowing water instead of lakes. DFO's category is therefore cut on the rearing habitat axis, where this concept and smn:SockeyeSeaTypeLifeHistory agree, and not on the duration axis, where they differ. A SER row cannot be resolved to a life-history type without population-level knowledge; the honest mapping of the code is to the species plus smn:RiverineRearingHabitat, which is what SER actually asserts, and not to this concept. This concept is also not chinook stream-type: the English is near-identical, the species differs, and the chinook term additionally carries an adult behavioural syndrome that this concept does not assert."
      }
    ],
    "https://w3id.org/smn/hasJuvenileFreshwaterResidenceDuration": [
      {
        "@id": "https://w3id.org/smn/YearlingFreshwaterResidence"
      }
    ],
    "https://w3id.org/smn/hasJuvenileRearingHabitat": [
      {
        "@id": "https://w3id.org/smn/RiverineRearingHabitat"
      }
    ]
  },
  {
    "@id": "https://w3id.org/smn/SockeyeSeaTypeLifeHistory",
    "@type": [
      "http://www.w3.org/2004/02/skos/core#Concept"
    ],
    "http://purl.obolibrary.org/obo/IAO_0000119": [
      {
        "@language": "en",
        "@value": "The ecotype label sea-type sockeye salmon: Wood, C.C., Riddell, B.E., and Rutherford, D.T. 1987, Can. Spec. Publ. Fish. Aquat. Sci. 96:12-24. The underlying scale-reading term sea type, and the phrase itself: Gilbert, C.H. 1913, Age at maturity of the Pacific coast salmon of the genus Oncorhynchus, Bulletin of the United States Bureau of Fisheries 32:1-22, page 8, in the Sockeye section. The freshwater-annulus discriminator, and the identification of Harrison River and Widgeon Slough as the sea-type populations: Beacham, T.D. and Withler, R.E. 2017, PLOS ONE 12(9):e0183713. The chinook rename that created the ocean-type ambiguity: Healey, M.C. 1991, Life history of chinook salmon (Oncorhynchus tshawytscha), in Groot, C. and Margolis, L. (eds.), Pacific Salmon Life Histories, UBC Press, page 314. Not Burgner 1991, which the 2026-08-17 draft cited here and which the citation record attaches to lake-type rather than to this concept."
      }
    ],
    "http://purl.org/dc/terms/source": [
      {
        "@id": "https://doi.org/10.1371/journal.pone.0183713"
      }
    ],
    "http://rs.tdwg.org/dwc/terms/scientificName": [
      {
        "@value": "Oncorhynchus nerka"
      }
    ],
    "http://rs.tdwg.org/dwc/terms/scientificNameID": [
      {
        "@value": "urn:lsid:marinespecies.org:taxname:254569"
      }
    ],
    "http://www.w3.org/2000/01/rdf-schema#isDefinedBy": [
      {
        "@id": "https://w3id.org/smn"
      }
    ],
    "http://www.w3.org/2000/01/rdf-schema#seeAlso": [
      {
        "@id": "https://www.marinespecies.org/aphia.php?p=taxdetails&id=254569"
      }
    ],
    "http://www.w3.org/2004/02/skos/core#altLabel": [
      {
        "@language": "en",
        "@value": "Ocean Type"
      },
      {
        "@language": "en",
        "@value": "Sea Type"
      }
    ],
    "http://www.w3.org/2004/02/skos/core#broader": [
      {
        "@id": "https://w3id.org/smn/LifeHistoryType"
      }
    ],
    "http://www.w3.org/2004/02/skos/core#definition": [
      {
        "@language": "en",
        "@value": "Life-history type of Sockeye Salmon in which juveniles migrate seaward during their first year, rearing for weeks to months in flowing water, sloughs, or the estuary without overwintering in fresh water, and so carry no freshwater annulus: subyearling freshwater residence combined with riverine rearing habitat."
      }
    ],
    "http://www.w3.org/2004/02/skos/core#historyNote": [
      {
        "@language": "en",
        "@value": "The 2026-08-17 draft of this concept asserted the opposite history, that sea-type was Gilbert's name for chinook and had been taken over by Sockeye Salmon. That is backwards and was replaced on 2026-08-25 after Gilbert (1913) was read: the term is introduced at page 8, inside the Sockeye section, and the chinook section at page 13 defines its types by reference to the Sockeye ones. The draft also asserted that this type has no rearing habitat worth naming; Beacham and Withler (2017) report that sea-type and river-type juveniles both rear in river habitats, so a habitat value is now asserted."
      }
    ],
    "http://www.w3.org/2004/02/skos/core#inScheme": [
      {
        "@id": "https://w3id.org/smn/LifeHistoryTypeScheme"
      }
    ],
    "http://www.w3.org/2004/02/skos/core#prefLabel": [
      {
        "@language": "en",
        "@value": "Sea-type Sockeye life history"
      }
    ],
    "http://www.w3.org/2004/02/skos/core#scopeNote": [
      {
        "@language": "en",
        "@value": "The discriminator is the freshwater annulus, not the label. Match on the definition, not on either string, because both strings are unsafe in different ways. \"Ocean Type\" is carried as an altLabel because DFO uses it for this concept, but it is AMBIGUOUS and must not be used as a matching key: DFO CSAS Res. Doc. 2017/074 declares river-type synonymous with ocean-type, which makes ocean-type the complement of lake-type and therefore broader than this concept, while DFO CSAS Res. Doc. 2023/003 uses ocean-type in exactly this concept's narrow sense and opposes it to river-type. Both are current. \"Sea-type\" is the older and more stable name: Gilbert (1913) coined sea type in his Sockeye Salmon section and applied it across five species, against a contrasting stream type, and the Sockeye sense has been continuous since. It was chinook that renamed — Healey (1991) designates the chinook form ocean-type, glossing it as Gilbert's sea-type — so a chinook ocean-type concept, if ever minted, must be a separate species-scoped IRI and is not a synonym of this one."
      }
    ],
    "https://w3id.org/smn/hasJuvenileFreshwaterResidenceDuration": [
      {
        "@id": "https://w3id.org/smn/SubyearlingFreshwaterResidence"
      }
    ],
    "https://w3id.org/smn/hasJuvenileRearingHabitat": [
      {
        "@id": "https://w3id.org/smn/RiverineRearingHabitat"
      }
    ]
  },
